  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA rather highly amplified upper trough, at least for summertime\nstandards, is in the process of pushing a surface cold front\nthrough the area at present. ISOLD to SCT showers continue \nin/near the higher terrain, with dry conditions elsewhere. \nVeering post-frontal flow should result in thickening / \nlowering stratus through the remainder of the overnight, \ngenerally limiting fog formation. Some patchy fog will remain \npossible, however, primarily in areas that received rainfall \nyesterday and had some clearing early, along with the higher \nterrain given low stratus.\n\nThe main change to the forecast over the last 36 hours or so has\nbeen a significant reduction in the chance for rain during the\nstart of the new work week (Monday/Tuesday). Following the\npassage of the front, much of the area is expected to remain dry\ntoday, with just the chance for ISOLD showers (~20-30%) and \nperhaps a rumble of thunder in the higher terrain given weak \nupslope flow. Mostly cloudy to overcast skies will break later \ntoday as drier air continues to filter in from the north as high\npressure builds into the area, giving way to a quiet night amid\na decent amount of valley fog.\n\nHigh pressure quickly slides northeast of the area on Tuesday,\nwith return flow and associated moisture advection bringing the\nchance for isolated to widely scattered showers/storms across \nthe higher terrain during the afternoon and evening hours, as a\ncutoff upper disturbance across the southeast CONUS gradually \nshifts north towards the forecast area. Additional flooding\nconcerns are not expected through Tuesday.\n\nHigh temperatures today will be ~ 5 degrees below normal, with\nseasonable temperatures expected tomorrow. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe aforementioned cutoff upper disturbance will gradually be\npulled northward Tuesday night into Thursday as a broad upper\ntrough across the north-central CONUS gradually progesses\neastward. This combined with increased warmth/moisture across \nthe area given continued return flow will result in increasing\nshower and storm chances beginning Wednesday onward, with the\nbest chance for rain being during the afternoon/evening hours \nwith diurnal heating (PoPs generally 40-70%). The pattern then \nremains active Friday into the weekend as a fairly \nzonal/progressive upper air pattern with weak crossing waves \nwill result in at least some daily shower and storm chances amid\nnear seasonable temperatures and higher humidity. The potential\nfor organized severe weather during this timeframe looks low, \nbut isolated stronger storms could occur, along with the \npotential for localized heavy rain.",
